diff --git a/doc/darwin-page-discard.md b/doc/darwin-page-discard.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..b6b2db3606
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/darwin-page-discard.md
@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+# Reclaim unused loom pages on macOS
+
+Darwin's `MADV_DONTNEED` did not release touched anonymous pages in a bounded
+probe. Large transient calculations could therefore retain their peak physical
+footprint after the nouns were freed. A restart reclaimed those pages.
+
+For the ordinary macOS loom, `u3e_toss()` now replaces complete unused pages
+with fresh anonymous mappings at the same addresses. The virtual reservation
+remains intact; physical storage is supplied again when reused. Live nouns,
+partial boundary pages, and the `PROT_NONE` guard are excluded. Every replaced
+page is marked dirty so later reuse is included in checkpointing, including
+pages that previously belonged to an older, larger snapshot.
+
+The range is validated before subtracting page counts. Empty, overlapping,
+reversed, and out-of-loom requests are ignored. Fixed-mapping failure is fatal:
+the process must not continue after a potentially damaged mapping. The swap
+shutdown caller now passes the correct retained south-page count, zero,
+instead of causing an unsigned underflow.
+
+Explicit `--swap` and other operating systems retain their existing advice
+backend. This change does not release live heap data, interior allocator holes,
+or live memo caches. It does not change the loom or checkpoint format.
+
+## Enable event-by-event reclamation
+
+Pass `--toss 0` to the main process. This enables `u3o_toss` and releases the
+unused heap/stack gap after events. The normal checkpoint path also calls
+`u3e_toss()`. The worker prints `--toss 0` even when tossing is disabled;
+verify that its `--runtime-config` includes bit 8192. Bit 128 alone does not
+enable tossing. Defaults remain unchanged.
+
+No reusable-page accounting protocol or allocator recommit hooks are needed.
+Replacing the gap with ordinary zero-fill mappings avoids dependence on
+`MADV_FREE_REUSABLE` / `MADV_FREE_REUSE` lifecycle coverage across every road,
+stack, directory, and post-event allocation path.
+
+## Verify
+
+Run one build at a time:
+
+```sh
+zig build toss-test palloc-test equality-test hashtable-test nock-test serial-test -j1 -Doptimize=ReleaseFast
+zig build toss-test -j1 -Doptimize=ReleaseFast -Dsnapshot-validation=true
+zig fmt --check build.zig
+git diff --check
+```
+
+`toss-test` uses real mappings and the actual snapshot implementation. It checks
+inward rounding, invalid ranges, boundary sentinels, guard protection, dirty
+bits, file-backed gap reuse, repeated physical-footprint and resident-memory
+contraction on macOS, direct live noun contents, both road directions, and
+snapshot reload with demand paging enabled and disabled. The reload test stays
+in-process; a disposable ship restart/replay is a separate integration check.
+
+The build enables `-Wall -Werror`. There is no separate configured C linter.
+Do not infer ship readiness from a successful build alone: validate the native
+connection, mount `%base`, verify its system exports, and check preserved app
+state and actual execution after deployment.
